iables  starting with the first axis and the  second  and the third  to check that these computational  relations are true let's use the  commutator of  x with px as an example  first we insert the definition of x in  terms of the original variables and  then the definition of px in terms of  the original variables  we can expand this communicator to get  first the common factors  and then four terms proportional to  various commutators  the first one between the position and  momentum of the first particle  the next is between the position of the  first particle and the momentum of the  second particle  next between the position of the second  particle and the momentum of the first  particle  and finally one between the position and  momentum  of the second particle we can cancel  this commutator and this commutator  because they are between different  particles so they're equal to zero  this one is i h bar and  so is this one putting everything  together the original commutator  finally gives i h bar and this confirms  this first computation relation here  the rest are equally simple and you can  work through the derivations yourselves  if you want to get some practice  overall we can work in terms of the  original coordinates given by this  collection of 12 operators obeying  three canonical computation relations or  in terms of this new set  of 12 operators which also obey the  corresponding canonical computation  relations  this type of change of variable is  extremely useful as we will see  in the rest of this video  we're now ready to look at the  hamiltonian of our system  we have the kinetic energy of particle  one the kinetic energy of particle two  and then their interaction energy which  depends on the relative position of the  two  particles before we move on we need an  important clarification about notation  if you've already seen the video on  tensor products  then you will know that this hamiltonian  acts on the state space w  of the two particle system which is  given by the tensor product  of the state space w1 of the first  particle  and the state space w2 of the second  particle  now when we spell out the hamiltonian  like this in terms of  three terms then each of these terms  must also  act on that same tensor product state  space  to be precise this means that the  kinetic energy operator of the first  particle  should really involve the tensor product  of p1  squared with the identity in w2  and the kinetic energy operator of the  second particle  should have the corresponding tensor  product  while these expressions involving tensor  products with identity operators  are the technically correct expressions  we typically use the shorthand  where we omit the identity operators and  the tensor product  and simply use this expression for  particle one  and this expression for particle 2.  this is the convention that i'm going to  use today moving forward  and only go back to the full expressions  when necessary  before we move on if you have not heard  about tensor products before  i would really encourage you to check  out our video on tensor products right  now  it's a very important topic when we  consider systems of more than  one quantum particle so for now let's  make some room and go back  to the problem at hand we want to  rewrite this hamiltonian in terms of the  center of mass and relative variables  for the kinetic energy terms let's first  consider the square of the momentum of  particle 1  using the expression that we derived  earlier about  this momentum in terms of the center of  mass and relative momentum  we can rewrite it like this we can now  calculate the square  and we get three terms one proportional  to the square of the center of mass  momentum  the other proportional to the relative  momentum  and then in principle we would get two  cross terms  but we can combine them into one because  the center of mass momentum variables  commute  with the relative momentum variables  using a similar derivation we can  rewrite  the square of the momentum of the second  particle as  a term proportional to the total  momentum squared  a term proportional to the relative  momentum squared and  a cross term we can now go back to our  full kinetic energy which is the sum of  the kinetic energy of particle one  and the kinetic energy of particle two  using the expressions that we just  derived  we start by combining the total momentum  terms here  and here we get this pre-factor  multiplied by the total momentum squared  next we combine the relative momentum  terms here and here  and we now get this pre-factor times the  relative momentum squared  the cross terms here and here cancel  because of this minus sign  and you might want to stop here for a  moment pause the video and just cross  check that you're happy with these tips  right moving on this here is the total  mass of the system  and this here is the reduced mass of the  system  putting everything together we can  rewrite our full hamiltonian in terms of  a kinetic energy term  that depends on the total mass of the  system and the total momentum squared  another kinetic energy term that depends  on  the reduced mass of the system and the  relative momentum squared  and we cannot forget the potential  energy term  which is trivial to rewrite in terms of  the new coordinates  its argument is simply the relative  coordinate  let's take a step back and see what  we've accomplished  by changing coordinates the original  hamiltonian has these three terms  when we rewrite it in terms of the new  center of mass  and relative coordinates we also get  three terms  in both cases we have two kinetic energy  terms associated with each type of  momentum  in the original expression the kinetic  energy terms depend on particle 1 of  mass  m1 here and particle 2 of  mass m2 here and in the new expression  we also have two kinetic energies the  first  one here looks like the kinetic energy  of a particle of  mass capital m the sum of the masses of  the original particles  and is given in terms of the center of  mass momentum  capital p the second here looks like the  kinetic energy of a particle of mass mu  the reduced mass of the two original  particles and depends  on the relative momentum this result is  really extremely  important looking at the kinetic  energies of the transformed hamiltonian  mathematically they look like the  kinetic energies of two particles  one of mass capital m and the other of  mass mu  so if this is what the math tells us we  can then also extend this to the  conceptual level  we can think of this system in terms of  the original  two real particles of masses m1 and  m2 or equivalently  in terms of two new fictitious particles  of masses m and mu  the question you will probably have at  this point is  why would we want to think in terms of  these  two fictitious particles rather than in  terms  of the original particles and the reason  is  the potential term while in the original  expression  the potential energy depended on both  position coordinates r1  and r2 the potential energy in the new  expression only depends on the relative  coordinate  r and this is critical because  while the two original particles  interact with each other  the two new fictitious particles do not  and this makes the solution of the  problem much simpler  because we've transformed it from a  problem of two real interacting  particles  to a problem of two fictitious  non-interacting particles in practice  this means that we can now  separate the hamiltonian into two  non-interacting terms  we define a center of mass hamiltonian  that only has a kinetic energy term  and we can define a relative hamiltonian  that has both a kinetic and a potential  energy term  the first hamiltonian describes a  fictitious particle of mass equal to the  sum of the masses of the two particles  and moving freely in space or to put it  another way  it describes the behavior of the center  of mass of the system  the second hamiltonian describes another  fictitious particle  whose mass is equal to the reduced mass  of the original particles  and is subject to this potential energy  it describes  the relative motion of the system about  the center of mass  the total hamiltonian is simply the sum  of these two hamiltonians and describes  the two fictitious particles  simultaneously  but they crucially don't interact with  each other  the next topic is therefore why  separable hamiltonians such as this one  are so much easier to work with than  hamiltonians  with an interaction term let's consider  a state-space wcom  which is the space where the center of  mass hamiltonian lives hence the acronym  com and let's also consider its  eigenvalue equation  where these are the eigenvalues and  these are the eigenstates  let's separately consider a space w  rel which is where the relative  hamiltonian lives  and this is its eigenvalue equation with  the eigenvalues here  and the eigenstates here  we can now construct the state space w  which is the tensor product of wcom with  w rail  the full hamiltonian h lives in this  tensor product space  and this is its eigenvalue equation  where these are the eigenvalues and  these are the eigenstates  what i want to show now is that we can  build  the eigenvalues and eigenstates of the  full hamiltonian here  from the eigenvalues and eigenstates of  the individual hamiltonians here and  here  which means that we can build the  solution of the two particle system  by only solving two separate one  particle systems  in particular we will show that we can  write the eigenstate psi  as the tensor product of the eigenstates  of the center of mass hamiltonian  and of the relative hamiltonian and that  we can write the total energy e  as the sum of the center of mass energy  and  the relative energy to see this  we will first show that the tensor  product state  is indeed the eigen state of the full  hamiltonian  we therefore start by writing the full  hamiltonian acting on the tensor product  state  we next expand the hamiltonian into two  separate terms  still acting on the tensor product state  and from the video on tensor products we  know that this term will  now only act on this part of the tensor  product  as this is simply the eigenvalue  equation for the center of mass  hamiltonian  we simply get the eigenvalue times the  state  similarly this term will only act on  this part of the tens product  and again it corresponds to the  eigenvalue equation of the relative  hamiltonian  so we get the corresponding eigenvalue  times  the state we can take out the tensor  product as a common factor  and we get this overall this confirms  that the tensor product state  is an eigenstate of the full hamiltonian  so we can indeed write the eigenstate  psi  as this tensor product and we also  immediately see  that the eigenvalue is simply the sum of  the eigenvalues of  the two separate hamiltonians here  overall this means that solving these  two separate eigenvalue problems is  enough to then build the solution to the  full hamiltonian up here  the eigenvalues are simply the sum of  the two original eigenvalues and the  eigen states are the tensor products  of the original eigenstates and this is  why  working in terms of the two fictitious  non-interacting particles  is more convenient than working in terms  of the real interacting particles  so we can solve the two parts of this  hamiltonian separately  let's start with the center of mass  hamiltonian here  we can write the hamiltonian as simply  given by the kinetic energy of the  center of mass  in the position representation the  kinetic energy term becomes this  and remember that the laplacian is  obtained from the gradient  and in the case of the center of mass  variables it is  explicitly given by this  putting things together we get this  eigenvalue equation in the positioned  representation  where i'm using the standard notation  for eigenfunctions  this is the eigenvalue equation of a  free particle of mass capital m  and from the corresponding video we know  that the eigenfunctions  are given by plane waves like this  and the eigen energies span a continuous  spectrum that has  a dispersion proportional to the  momentum squared  overall this means that the center of  mass behaves like a free particle  and this hamiltonian simply describes  translations of the system  as a whole with all the energy being in  the form  of kinetic energy we can therefore solve  the  behavior of the center of mass once and  for all  and we've just done it  let's now turn to the relative  hamiltonian here if we copy it  we now remember that this hamiltonian  has a kinetic energy part  and a potential energy part and again  working in the position representation  we can rewrite it like this  the laplacian is again obtained from the  gradient which in this case of the  relative variables is given  by this expression putting everything  together we get  this eigenvalue equation in the position  representation  unlike the case of the center of mass  hamiltonian  for the eigenvalue equation of the  relative hamiltonian  we cannot provide a once and for all  solution  this is because of this potential term  here which will take a different form  for different systems so to fully solve  the problem of two interacting quantum  particles  we've now gone as far as we can go  without specifying the form  of the interaction potential for  specific examples i refer you to our  videos  linked in the description for example  those on the hydrogen atom  where this interaction is given by the  coulomb potential  however before we conclude there is in  fact one special case  in which we can take one extra step  and this is the case when the potential  v  does not depend generally on the  relative position of the two particles  but in fact only depends on their  relative  distance and by that i mean the  magnitude  of the relative coordinate at this point  you will probably all recognize this  potential it is the central potential  this means that what we learned about  the motion for single particle in the  central potential  in the corresponding videos can be  directly used  to describe the relative motion of two  particles interacting  via a potential that only depends on the  distance between the two particles  you may wonder whether this is the  situation that we ever encounter  and the answer is a resounding yes  the coulomb potential is an example of  an interaction between two particles  that only depends on the distance  between the particles  a very good way to see this in action is  to check out our videos on the hydrogen  atom  in which a proton and an electron  interact precisely via  the coulomb potential from our  discussion today  this means that although the hydrogen  atom is a two particle problem  the relevant physics described by the  relative motion of the proton  and the electron it's effectively a one  particle problem  and furthermore it is the problem of one  particle  moving in a central potential  so overall we've arrived at a rather  neat result  we started with a system of two  complicated  interacting quantum particles in which  the interaction  only depends on the relative position of  the two particles  we've then found that we can separate  this into two different problems  one for the motion of one fictitious  particle which moves as a free particle  whose mass is a total mass of the system  and  which describes the center of mass  motion of the system  and the second as the motion of another  fictitious particle  whose mass is the reduced mass of the  system  which describes the relative motion of  the two original particles  this is pretty useful because rather  than having to solve the original  problem of two interacting particles  we now have two separate problems of two  non-interacting fictitious particles  which are  much easier to solve today we've laid  the groundwork to study systems of two  interacting particles as a good example  of such a system i would encourage you  to  watch our video on the hydrogen atom and  as always if you liked the video please  subscribe
 

